WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.850
SAAD ALI: Hello, folks.

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:01.590
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.214
I'm a software engineer
here at Google.

00:00:03.214 --> 00:00:04.890
And I'm joined here by Mace.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.444
Mace is an SRE on the GKE team.

00:00:07.444 --> 00:00:08.610
JENNIFER MACE: That's right.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.040
SAAD ALI: Macy, can you
explain what an SRE is?

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.100
JENNIFER MACE: So SRE stands
for a Site Reliability Engineer.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.100
And the main difference between
me and any other software

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.110
engineer is that I have more
of a focus on reliability,

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.040
scalability, and just
general efficiency

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.550
of the running of the thing
that I'm owning, which is GKE.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.090
SAAD ALI: So what does that
mean, when you own GKE?

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.640
Are you responsible
for running it?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.140
What do you do for customers?

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:31.973
JENNIFER MACE: Sure.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:36.720
So the idea is that we set
SLOs, which are Service Level

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.140
Objectives, which are
measurements of how reliable we

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.150
want the running service to be.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.311
And my job is to do any
kind of engineering,

00:00:44.311 --> 00:00:45.810
really, that's
required to make sure

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.182
that we're meeting those goals.

00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:47.890
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.848
So how does this differ
from a traditional kind

00:00:49.848 --> 00:00:50.797
of DevOps role?

00:00:50.797 --> 00:00:52.380
JENNIFER MACE: So I
think that there's

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.560
a lot of interplay
between DevOps and SRE.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.920
They're fairly tightly coupled.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:57.742
SAAD ALI: OK.

00:00:57.742 --> 00:00:59.700
And in terms of some of
the challenges that you

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.460
see in your day to day
job, what are they?

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.680
Can you talk about that?

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:04.513
JENNIFER MACE: Sure.

00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:06.870
So I think that one of the
big challenges for GKE,

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.770
which is a massive
hosted Kubernetes system,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.920
is really just the
diversity of use cases.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.970
We run a five-digit
number of clusters.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.077
And every user has
slightly different needs

00:01:21.077 --> 00:01:22.660
and slightly different
configurations,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.229
so we're constantly seeing
new challenges crop up.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:27.270
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.340
So how do you actually
deal with them?

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.380
Are there best practices?

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.150
Do you have any
suggestions for customers?

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.010
Or do you take care of
most of the pain for them?

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.400
JENNIFER MACE: So we try to
take care of anything that's

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.530
on the platform level
and anything that

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.980
is really internal, so that
anything that a user can't take

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.029
action to fix is super
frustrating for them,

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:48.070
and we don't want them to
have to deal with that.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.560
But, in general,
the philosophy of

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.110
SRE to reduce the time to
mitigate a broken system.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.770
So we may not have a perfect
fix or perfectly understand

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.390
the problem, but
our goal is always

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.050
to make the user able to
use their system again fast,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.600
and then figure it out after
the fact with postmortems.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.620
SAAD ALI: What
happened, and make sure

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.520
that it doesn't happen again.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.139
JENNIFER MACE: Exactly.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.000
And wherever possible, to build
systems to do that, and build

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.940
it into the code, rather than
relying on humans to come in

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.960
and fix something.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:16.800
SAAD ALI: Very cool.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.662
And so, when we were
talking off-camera,

00:02:18.662 --> 00:02:21.120
you were mentioning some really
interesting design patterns

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.130
that SRE deals with.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.667
Can you talk about that?

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:25.500
JENNIFER MACE: Sure.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.736
So one of the challenges in
hosting a massive deployment

00:02:29.736 --> 00:02:31.860
of Kubernetes clusters,
which I talked a little bit

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.950
about earlier, is that
the traditional SRE

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.440
model for a
hyperscalable system is

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.370
to build a multi-tenant system.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.020
And what that means is that
all of your query flow and user

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.940
action is flowing
through a single binary,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.970
or a single replicated
set of binaries.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.750
Now, a more traditional
monolith for a single user

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.500
is what we call a
single tenant system.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.080
So for example, an
Oracle database.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.270
It has one user,
and it's completely

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.290
specified for that user, and
it's been tweaked and perfected

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.020
for exactly their use
case, but it's really

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.330
only useful to one
person at once.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.300
The challenge in hosting
these Kubernetes clusters

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.050
is that every single Kubernetes
cluster is, in its own way,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.020
from my point of view,
a single tenant system.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.050
You know, this system
belongs to one user.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.910
This system belongs to
a different customer.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.909
They don't have any
interaction with each other.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:26.010
And as an operator,
I can't really

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.630
treat them as a monolith.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.844
So we're trying to develop some
new operations patterns for how

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:34.260
we deal with that, and keep
them all running, when they're

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.530
so different from one another.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.220
And that's what we've been
calling, starting to call,

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.152
multi single tenant systems.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:40.860
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.036
Is this a pattern that
customers could potentially

00:03:43.036 --> 00:03:44.160
apply in their own systems?

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.780
JENNIFER MACE: Absolutely.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.230
Whenever you have a customer
who is building something that

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:54.090
has many separate users, a
pipeline, or a shop deployment,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.740
or anything along
those lines, there

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.510
are very similar
patterns to keep in mind.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.884
SAAD ALI: And it
definitely helps

00:03:58.884 --> 00:04:00.480
with scalability
of their solutions.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:01.480
JENNIFER MACE: For sure.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.910
I think one of
the key principles

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.730
that we're starting to come up
with is really that automation

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:09.870
is king, and that monitoring
and trying to make sure

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.180
that you can have
some equivalent

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.970
monitoring between each of
your single tenant systems,

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.279
because it doesn't
matter whether you

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:19.829
are using your Kubernetes
cluster to host

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:22.079
a machine learning
pipeline and I'm

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.600
using mine to host a
YouTube front page.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.650
Both of us have a control
plane that has uptime.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.840
And it's meaningful to
measure control plane uptime,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.202
and I can compare our
two clusters with that.

00:04:33.202 --> 00:04:34.660
SAAD ALI: That's
super interesting.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.493
Let's take a step back
and talk a little bit

00:04:36.493 --> 00:04:38.700
about your background
and how you got into SRE.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.220
JENNIFER MACE: So I
joined Google in 2012

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.840
as a new graduate with
a maths and CS degree.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.314
And I went straight into
the Google Ads SRE team.

00:04:47.314 --> 00:04:48.730
Kind of learned
everything I know.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:50.010
SAAD ALI: Did you know that
you wanted to be in SRE.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.093
JENNIFER MACE: Not at all.

00:04:51.093 --> 00:04:53.760
I had offers from three
separate teams, and one of them

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:54.360
was an SRE.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.651
And I went to some of my
friends at Google, and I said,

00:04:56.651 --> 00:04:57.510
what's an SRE?

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.910
And they said, well, they
wear leather jackets,

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:04.600
and they swear a
lot, and they're

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.557
always really on the
go, and stressed out.

00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:08.890
And I'm like, that sounds great.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:09.920
Sign me up.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:10.670
SAAD ALI: Oh, man.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.580
And it seems like
you've enjoyed it since.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:13.030
JENNIFER MACE: Yeah.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:13.905
I'm having great fun.

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:16.405
SAAD ALI: What do you recommend
for new grads who are trying

00:05:16.405 --> 00:05:17.690
to follow a similar journey?

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.189
What should they
look into, and what

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:20.694
are the kind of
things that can help

00:05:20.694 --> 00:05:22.360
them determine whether
to follow the SRE

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.770
path or a different path?

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:24.880
JENNIFER MACE: Well,
one of the things

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.180
that they might want to have
a look at is the SRE book.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.971
We recently came out
with the SRE workbook,

00:05:28.971 --> 00:05:30.940
and I actually have
a write-up of one

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.310
of the big GKE outages
in that workbook,

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.860
if people are curious.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.990
And another thing is just
try spinning up some systems,

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.705
and try breaking them.

00:05:42.705 --> 00:05:44.080
Don't just try to
make them work.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.760
Try to break them, and
figure out how they break.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.140
And if that's
interesting to you,

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.550
then this might be
an area to pursue.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:50.110
SAAD ALI: Yeah, I
think there might

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.900
be a lot of folks out there
that are interested in that.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:52.817
So thank you for that.

00:05:52.817 --> 00:05:55.108
Let's switch gears a little
bit and talk about KubeCon.

00:05:55.108 --> 00:05:56.390
This is your first KubeCon.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:56.650
JENNIFER MACE: Yeah.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:57.800
SAAD ALI: What are
your impressions?

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.675
JENNIFER MACE: It's huge.

00:05:58.675 --> 00:05:59.650
SAAD ALI: Yes, it is.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:01.600
JENNIFER MACE: It's
really, really big.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.510
I was a little surprised about
the gender diversity here.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:11.510
My SRE team is about half women,
which is a little bit unusual,

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:14.620
but I was not expecting it to
be quite as masculine as it--

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:15.730
SAAD ALI: Male-dominated.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:16.630
JENNIFER MACE: --as it is here.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:17.410
Yeah.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.540
SAAD ALI: What can we
do to improve that?

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:21.498
JENNIFER MACE: I mean,
I think that it's really

00:06:21.498 --> 00:06:25.030
a question of looking at
how the community treats

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.260
various people.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.510
I know that in the past,
I've had some challenges

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.520
with a very feminine
name in open source,

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:31.990
getting code reviews.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.760
It feels like you got
a bit more pushback.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.220
But also just one of the
things that I did notice

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.050
that KubeCon is
working on is having

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.350
technical women on stage.

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:39.850
Right?

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.090
All of the keynotes
have had a huge number

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:46.844
of really impressive
women, like Janet and Liz.

00:06:46.844 --> 00:06:48.510
And that's something
I super appreciate.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.820
SAAD ALI: That is awesome.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.810
And finally, let's wrap up with,
what are you looking forward

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:53.930
to most at KubeCon?

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.605
Is there a talk or whatever?

00:06:56.605 --> 00:06:58.480
JENNIFER MACE: Honestly,
I am looking forward

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.480
to talking to other people who
are using Kubernetes in ways

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:02.271
that we are not.

00:07:02.271 --> 00:07:02.770
Right?

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.380
I feel like that's what
I get the most out of,

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.450
is one to one
conversations, figuring out

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.014
where people's pain
points are, how

00:07:09.014 --> 00:07:10.180
they're different from mine.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.880
Because I have a bit of a weird
perspective on Kubernetes.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.260
I don't use Kubernetes.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:15.470
I run Kubernetes.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:15.970
Right?

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:16.810
So it's a bit of a--

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:17.530
SAAD ALI: And if
we can figure out

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.930
how customers are using it
in ways that we don't expect,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.629
we can make it more robust
and reliable over time.

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:23.920
JENNIFER MACE: That's the idea.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.540
SAAD ALI: That is
the role of SRE.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.410
Thank you very much, Mace.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.451
JENNIFER MACE: Thank you.

00:07:27.451 --> 00:07:29.660
SAAD ALI: It was a pleasure
talking to you likewise.

